    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1936, the Bureau of Indian Affairs chose the site as the Central Agency Headquarters, consolidating scattered Navajo agencies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Window-Rock",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Window Rock serves as the capital of the Navajo Nation, hosting the council, supreme court, and presidential offices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Window_Rock%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The sandstone window is one of four sacred sites where Navajo medicine men fetch water for the traditional Water Way Ceremony.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Window_Rock%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Navajo Nation Museum and its adjacent library showcase artifacts, language resources, and ceremonies central to Navajo heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
